A||N||31||Bigfieroman||I just almost killed myself in my fiero.||||9||
Z||000000||Bigfieroman||08-14-2001||10:34 PM||||I was on my favorite road, in the backwoods of a local park.  I went through once, through the long hilly strait, brakes and then a decreasing radius corner.  On the way home, I was passing the entrance to the route again, and descided to try it again even though I kind of had a bad feeling about it.  5 mph faster this time, up to 95, my fastest in this area.  I braked hard for the corner, but wanted to take it faster so not as much as last time.  The rear end let go early, under a little braking, as it sometimes does.  I began to countersteer a little when the front suddenly let go as well.  It was all over before I knew it.  I went airborne off the road edge at 65 mph, and began sliding sideways down the embankment (about 30 deg).  Using everything I learned from Gran Turismo, I let off the brakes a little to dodge the 100' trees in my tangent.  As I slid my tires dug in and I slowed very quickly, Almost rolling over in the process.  I stopped 300' from the road, driver side door first, about 1' from a huge oak.  In fact, I was so close I had to get out the passenger side to catch my breath.  Everything was fine.  The engine was still running, the tranny still in gear, all the lights still on.  I caught my breath, and slowly drove back up the hill, back onto the road, and continued at about 10 mph to a local pavillion with flood lights.  Looking at my car, the only evidence of anything happening was a little dirt on the driver side tires.  Amazing.  I drove back to where I was, and realized that this was the only place I could have gone off and survived, or at least gotten out free of damage.  Earlier in the turn, and the embankment is 60 deg, and fully wooded.  Later in the turn, and there is a gaurd rail, followed by a cliff.  I suppose you can chalk up another near-victim to bump-steer.  Needless to say, I will NEVER drive that fast around a turn again unless I am on a road course somewhere.||||reg||9
Z||000001||Bigfieroman||08-14-2001||10:36 PM||||I forgot to mention I was on my way back from a Fiero club meeting where I had discussed my intentions to revamp my entire suspension.||||reg||1
Z||000002||FieroKyle||08-14-2001||10:48 PM||||I know that Feeling. I was going through a park at about 11:00 at night. There are set of high banked corners with recc. speeds of 20 to 30 mph. Well nobody is around here at all past 10:00 so I decided to go around as fast I can. I usually fly anywhere from 80 to 85 and just squeel the tires going around them with great traction. (Goodyear Eagles GT2 215s 14). Well, this night I got gutsy and was flying about 85 to 90. On the last corner (a left) I dived down into the other lane at 95 mph. Slowed it down with the brakes a bit going in and as soon as I hit the inside lane, wham the back end came right around on me and I locked up the brakes then missed the inside gaurd rail by foot or less while spinning then the back end started to climb the enbankment. The wham, I hit a low cement wall. Didn't do anydamage except it lowered my left exhaust tip about 6 inches which I fixed the following day. What I think happened, A. I got on the brakes to late entering the corner, or B. I ran over a couple of bumps which gave me wheel hop which spun it right around. Scared me half to death spinning like that. Afterwards when I learned of no damage, it really then came as a rush more than a piss in my pants.||||reg||1
Z||000003||California Kid||08-14-2001||11:01 PM||||Man......you're lucky you're not in the hospital!!!!<P>You never, I mean never go into a turn hot with a Fiero, unless you know exactly what you're doing, how the car will react, and know the road like the back of your hand.<P>The worse thing you can do with a Fiero in this situation is lean on the brakes!  You have to pick a point in front of the turn to just tap the brake pedal (once) slightly, setting the nose down alittle before entering the turn(weight transfer to front tires, even out front to rear distribution of weight). If the back end appears to be drifting out, you have to add just a little power (again, a little transfer of weight to the rear to balance it out). If this doesn't work, you've completely mis-calculated what the car is capable of, or something broke.<P>No flame intended or implied, you only learn these things from talking to people, performance driving school, or self experience hard knocks.<P>Happy to hear your OK and the car isn't damaged.<p>[This message has been edited by California Kid (edited 08-15-2001).]||||reg||4
Z||000004||Raydar||08-14-2001||11:08 PM||||Jeez, guys (and ladies)!<BR>Seems like I've been reading about a lot of "close calls" and "near misses", lately.<P>Please be careful. I hate hearing about people's cars getting bent. I really hate to hear about someone getting injured or worse.<P>Not to sound preachy, just be careful. <BR>You are all you've got.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||9
Z||000005||aka||08-14-2001||11:34 PM||||I don't know if its a common occurance or not but I've done the same thing with my 85sc.  A buddy and I were going up an offramp, posted speed limit was 40 and we were doing alittle over 100 (km/h)  I had never lost traction before and thought the car was invincible but the rear end let loose and we did a 270 with the nose in the ditch.  Thankfully there was no drop, only grass to slide on.  Is it just my car or do the rear wheels let loose without a sound?  My car just slid sideways like it was on ice...   Anyways, I do take it seriously, I don't do it anymore but it did show me the limits of my car.  ||||reg||1
Z||000006||artherd||08-15-2001||01:20 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by aka:<BR><B>I I had never lost traction before and thought the car was invincible</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>EVERY car I have ever owned gets driven to an abandon parking lot just after I buy it.  There, I take as long as I need (sometimes several hours) getting the car up to, and beyond, it's limits.  Learn it inside and out. what happens, and what to do to stay in control.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B><BR>Is it just my car or do the rear wheels let loose without a sound?  My car just slid sideways like it was on ice... </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Welcome to the world of mid-engine cars.  Their limits are higher than any other layout, but when they are exceed, they become POSATIVELY reinforced.<P>Ie, once the tyres break, if you don't *actively correct* the matter, and I mean do the exact right thing NOW, you will end up in the ditch.  The car will not settle itself if you panic and let up.  It will keep sliding, and it will get worse.<P>I could offer all kinds of advice, but what everyone who drives a mid-engine (or any) car needs to do is find this abandoned parking lot, and just drive.  Fast. and lose control, and figure out how to keep it.    Go back every week or two or three and keep yourself refreshed.  <BR>And maybe even a little scared, a little fear is good, it keeps you aware.<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000007||MrPBody||08-15-2001||08:54 AM||||Yep, a lot of that going around . . . and around, and around.  Maybe it's a rotavirus.  I wonder if there's a vaccine for it.<P>These things do turn into a handful when they come unstuck!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Springtyme||08-15-2001||09:12 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>I will NEVER drive that fast around a turn again unless I am on a road course somewhere.<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Eric???? Did you read this???  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR>            ~Springtyme~||||reg||11
Z||000009||fierohoho||08-15-2001||09:48 AM||||Slow down before the curve and then accelerate through it.<P>You lost it when you began braking in the turn, does it every time.<P>Glad your OK, would have liked to have seen your face though <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><P>------------------<BR>'84 SE 2.5l<BR>'85 Coupe 2.5l now getting a 3800<BR>'86 SE 2.5l||||reg||1
Z||000010||stsmithgt||08-15-2001||09:54 AM||||Boy does that sound familiar.  Mine wasn't that fast or sliding that far, but those trees, slope and air sure sound familiar.  I have new shocks and struts to keep me on the road now.  Well, if my clutch system didn't fail.||||reg||1
Z||000011||stimpy||08-15-2001||10:05 AM||||Wow, that sounds uncannily like what happened to <B>me</B>! I was drivng past the local day care slaloming through boxes of kittens at about 140. I guess I should have known better, because it had been sleeting steadily all morning. Well, surely enough, as I navigated the last box of kittens, a moose walked onto the street! I could feel the car become airborne as I coolly made evasive manuevers. My car shot across the daycare centers playground, and using the skills I have acquired from playing Ms. PacMan, I was able to avoid all the kids playing. I came to rest in the sandbox, the engine still purring an the radio playing Frank Sinatra singing "My Way".  Upon closer inspection I saw that the little carpet crawlers in the playground had actually applied a gleaming coat of Zaino durig my flight.<P>Boy, was I relieved!||||reg||1
Z||000012||AusFiero||08-15-2001||10:58 AM||||LOL stimpy. I notice you didn't mention the nun that was in the sandpit playing with the girlscouts helping out at the day care centre that day.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/ausfiero1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero</A> | <A HREF="http://www.auto-alley.com" TARGET=_blank>Fiero Accessories</A>||202.139.20.3||reg||1
Z||000013||Wolfhound||08-15-2001||01:47 PM||||A tree tried to kill my Blue GT yesterday. I drove it to the house I was working on, here in town. When I came out to leave, a dead oak had dropped a 10 foot, 12 inch diameter limb about 2 foot away from the drivers side door and parallel to the car.<BR>Freaked me out, the paint is only a few weeks old.   ||||reg||11
Z||000014||DRH||08-15-2001||02:16 PM||||stimpy, ROFLMMFAO... great one!<P>People be careful out there. Mistakes made at 95 MPH out in the real world are too often the last mistake for somebody.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000015||84Bill||08-15-2001||02:39 PM||||I saw it all stimpy! Luckly the school bus had just turned off it's flashing red lights when you passed it at 90 mph.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000016||Ken Wittlief||08-15-2001||02:41 PM||||thats the trouble with driving on a familiar road - i took the turn last time at 70 so this time ill try 75 and see what happens.<P> you get suckered into a situation you cant get back out of - this is ok on a closed race track when the last time you took the curve was 2 minutes ago - but out on an open road you dont know what has changed since the last time you were there:<P> sand or dirt or leaves on the pavement - animals or people or cars in the road - damage to the pavement...<P>  when you are out on the open roads, drive according to what you can see, not based on what happened last time you were there - like some of the others have suggested - get into the turns at a controlled speed, and then push it back up from there based on what is happening NOW - not based on what happened on the same road a week ago.<P>  If you push your car up to its limits in a curve you can feel whats happening, and you can stay in contol - even if that means a 4 wheel power drift - if you throw your car into a curve beyond its limitations, there is not a damn thing you can do about it - you're going offroading!||||reg||10
Z||000017||87GTBro||08-15-2001||03:18 PM||||So let me get this straight.  I need a driving lesson here.  Where I live, it hardly ever rains, so there is dust everywhere.  But when it does rain, that dust turns into a slimy mess on the road.<P>If I am going around a turn and lose the rear in 2nd gear (not driving fast) what is the best thing to do?  <P>A) Turn into the slide and press on the accelerator?<P>or<P>b) Turn into the slide and press on the brake?<P>My instinct would be to press on the brake.  Is this the wrong thing to do?  Call me inexperienced!<P>------------------<BR>AOL IM: GTFiero86Dan<BR>'86 GT 4 speed<BR>'87 GT Auto<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/87GTBrosig1.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000018||Ken Wittlief||08-15-2001||04:13 PM||||usually if you lose the rear - its too late<P>when you are going around a turn near the cars limits think of yourself as being in a boat or aircraft, more than in a car.<P>  it depends on whether the turn is getting tighter, staying the same, or getting wider, but in general keep the front wheels pointed where you want the car to go, and use the power of the motor and the back wheels to push the car back towards the center of the curve - and if possible head the car a little wider on the turn.<P>  there are so many variables its hard to set a rule that will cover all situations - you really need to get out and skid around curves in a power drift and learn how it feels, and experiment, so you will know if you should lean on the gas to push the car through, or aim higher in the turn and let off slightly to get the rear straighten out again.<P>  If you keep the steering wheel where it is when the rear starts to go, and let off the gas, or worse, brake, your gonna do a 180.<P>  you almost need to develope a differnt way of driving, like the car is sliding all the time, and learn how to control it while its sliding. it feels like you are piloting a hovercraft - but once you get a feel for it, it becomes second nature.||||reg||10
Z||000019||stsmithgt||08-15-2001||05:59 PM||||In my favorite 270° loop of an off ramp that lures me in at a high rate of speed time to time, I lost the back end way more than I expected.  I found myself letting of the gas while turning into the slide.  I got back into the gas when I felt enough controll come back to power the rest of the way through the curve.  A nice rush at 6:00 a.m..  I figure I'd get some of that forward motion of my rearend coming around reduced before grabbing for traction for the rest of the curve.  If that back end is coming around fast, don't expect to stop it with a little twitch of the steering wheel while your foot is still in the gas.  These aren't OUTLAW sprint cars. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000020||Bigfieroman||08-15-2001||06:34 PM||||I am going back tonight with my digital camera to take some pics.  Ill post them tonight.<P>------------------<BR>I once got a warning from a Police officer because he couldn't keep up with me around some twisties.  the prerequisite for a warning instead of a ticket was that I must let him drive it back and forth the way we had come.  Burned a quater tank of my gas!||||reg||1
Z||000021||Bigfieroman||08-15-2001||07:56 PM||||Here is the 95mph straight:<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/straight.JPG"> <BR>The braking area:<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/brakes_1.JPG"> <BR>The view that I had before going down:<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/overhill.JPG"> <BR>The tracks, (2 pics):<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/down2nd.JPG"> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/down3rd.JPG"> <BR>My final resting place (that could have worked on 2 levels):<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/finalrest.JPG"> <BR>The "damage":<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/damage.JPG"> <BR>Quite amazing.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000022||fierohoho||08-15-2001||10:21 PM||||Nice dash!<P>------------------<BR>'84 SE 2.5l<BR>'85 Coupe 2.5l now getting a 3800<BR>'86 SE 2.5l||||reg||1
Z||000023||Master_Sushi||08-16-2001||12:38 AM||||Wow, that looks like a scarey place to lose control of a car at 95. Well just about anywhere is a bad place to lose control at 95  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">. Glad everything came out allright for you! I have never lost control at those speeds before. I have span out my fiero a few times while doing some fast driving though. Most ever was doing about 50 or so around some sharp curvs on country roads. I noticed with my fiero setup, handeling seems a little diff then most ive read about. I just have a stock 88 coupe suspention, with new stock shocks/struts/bushings. But I have 16 inch wheels with Kumho rubber and larger series tire in back.<BR>Anyway, when maxing out around a corner, I notice the front end starts sliding a little bit. I can still stear a little more and I still have control. But then if I keep on it the back end starts to lose grip. The car now feels like it is evenly sliding sideways.Then all of a sudden the back end will almost snap out of control on me. This happens very quickly. I can sometimes counterstear and  slide through with the back end out, but this is very tricky because it happens so fast. When I had my stock 14 inch wheels/tires this condition was worse.<BR>Driving the mid engine platform is much better hendeling but as other have said, when you lose it your in trouble  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">.<P><P>------------------<BR>88 Fiero Coupe...<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Mesopotamis_1.jpg"> ||||reg||4
Z||000024||fiero56||08-16-2001||01:01 AM||||I thought my '86SE was the only Fiero with major handling problems. My '86 is the only pre-'88 I've really driven, and I have yet to get comfortable with the feel(squirrel-y-ness) of it. It seems too easy to push beyond its capabilities, and that rear end is very "lively". I have never experienced this feeling driving my Formula. I have yet to have the rear end even feel like attempting to break loose unless I punch the throttle. I guess my squirrely '86 isn't so abnormal.||||reg||1
Z||000025||86SE||08-16-2001||01:38 AM||||Ahhh, yes.  That curve remindes me of a curve near my house.  90 degree turn with 20 degree (or so) embankment.  I decided to test my cars handling one day just flying back and fourth increasing my speed by 5 MPH each time around.  First time 85, "WOW, awesome gravitational force" I thought.  So turn around and go back the other way at 90, "Even better!"  Heard no squeeling tires at all, I was very impressed.  Next time around, 95 miles an hour.  Hmmm......lets push it now.  I was trying to slowly work my way upto 120 but I never got there.  On my pre 120 attempt, I ran 105 MPH around the corner, slow when I get to it, and BAM, punch it when I was in the turn, no tire squeel, I thought "hey, im alright"  then the car just dropped when the embankment ended.  I started in the inside lane, and ended up almost in the ditch clear on the other side of the road.  It felt like the car actually flew off the ground, hell of a feeling, but I never want to feel that again.<P>The design of the embankment was perfect goin south, slowly tilted back to flat pavement at high speeds, but going north, the embankment just drops at 100+ i found out....scared the hell outta me if you can imagine, Fiero would have been toasted in a field after being rolled and flipped a few times..<P>I learned my driving skills from Carmageddon.<P>Oh yeah, regular flat corners (dead intersections) I have taken upto 45 MPH (fastest).  I was pulling into work, had smoke rolling off my left front tire (right handed turn).  Now that was just neat.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by 86SE (edited 08-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000026||Bigfieroman||08-16-2001||12:27 PM||||It isn't that my car has problems, it usually handles quite predictably.  I guess I was just going too fast for either end to hold on, and I got lucky.  I still like that turn with the decreasing radius, but I belive I will stick to the 20 mph rec speed.||||reg||1
Z||000027||falconhulk||08-16-2001||03:19 PM||||fiero56, I think you misundrstood what was being said!  People are not saying that their handling is bad but that once you push it beyond its limits its a bear to get back under control.  I have an 85 and it handles better then any car I ever drove.  If yours has problems its just that a problem.  You should look into it and get it fixed.||||reg||1
Z||000028||fiero56||08-16-2001||06:39 PM||||Nope, I understood. MY '86 handles well under normal/moderate driving. But once you throw some odd angles, or manuvers at it, it is one wild ride(I do the parking lot tests after I buy a car). My '86 will break the rear end loose under less force than it takes to push the front end of my Formula.<P>How many of you pre-'88ers(with stock suspension) can grab the steering wheel at 50mph, and sling the car left to right to left several times and never have the rear end want to come around on you(NOT recommended!)(my formula could do that all day and never have a problem, I did it once in my '86 and was lucky I was able to catch it in time)? I hope to never be in a situation while in my '86 where I need to avoid something quickly. Motorweek video even has same complaint. I thought the MW video was a bit biased against the Fiero until I drove an earlier model myself. I like 'em, but they need tweaking that Pontiac didn't do. At this point, I'm planning to sell the '86 in a few months.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 08-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000029||MrPBody||08-16-2001||10:03 PM||||fiero56 --<P>Having just done <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/009455.html" TARGET=_blank>that very thing</A> at about 70mph, I gotta say that you have a point.  And my suspension isn't quite stock.<P>That's one reason I want a Formula.  Maybe next year.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by MrPBody (edited 08-16-2001).]||||reg||1
